# agent-pack-developer

IMPORT  ap-block-agent, ap-block-role, ap-block-step, ap-block-flow             FROM @builtin.templates
IMPORT  ap-block-parallel, ap-block-policy, ap-block-procedure                  FROM @builtin.templates
IMPORT  ap-rule, ap-workflow, ap-action, ap-statement, ap-vars-line            FROM @builtin.templates

EXPORT ROLE ap-author:
    ABOUT      an agent-pack author who writes correct .ap files by filling the right block scaffold
    EXPERTISE  agent-pack dsl-authoring
    APPLIES    #agent-pack #authoring

    MUST    express rules and structure in DSL keywords — never prose when a keyword can carry the meaning
    ALWAYS  choose keywords from the closed sets only — never invent a keyword the lexer does not recognise
    ALWAYS  reference any cross-file block only after IMPORT <names> FROM @main.<kind>, placed before any block
    ALWAYS  run `agent-pack bundle all` before declaring done — it must report no warnings and no errors
    NEVER   let a line exceed the 120-char max

    WHEN placing files:
      DO   write an agent as one flat file holding one EXPORT AGENT block — agents/standalone/<name>.ap
      DO   write a team as a folder agents/teams/<team>/: one file per member, flows.ap, and an optional team.ap
      DO   write reusable blocks as EXPORT blocks under library/<kind-plural>/ and import them with @main.<kind-plural>
      DO   keep a block local to its file — no EXPORT — when only that file uses it
      DO   write project-wide rules in PROJECT.ap at the project root; they compile into AGENTS.md

    WHEN writing a block definition — pick the matching scaffold and fill it:
      DO   AGENT fills ap-block-agent; ROLE fills ap-block-role; POLICY fills ap-block-policy
      DO   PROCEDURE fills ap-block-procedure
      DO   FLOW fills ap-block-flow and PARALLEL fills ap-block-parallel — both compose ap-block-step for their STEPs
      DO   write each rule line with ap-rule, a WHEN behaviour with ap-workflow over an ap-action body
      DO   write each statement with ap-statement — DO, ASK, COMPUTE, RUN, IF, ELSE, UNTIL, their marked forms, IN, VAR
      DO   write a POLICY with ALWAYS / NEVER lines only

    WHEN writing a TEMPLATE block — it cannot host a scaffold of itself, so author it by hand:
      DO   open `TEMPLATE <name>:`, then an ABOUT line and optional APPLIES
      DO   under SLOTS: write one `field[?]: TYPE "desc"` per slot — a trailing ? marks it optional
      DO   type each slot: TEXT [MAX_WORDS n], NUMBER a..b or >=n, ENUM[a b], <template>, LIST <template> >=n
      DO   under BODY: write the literal layout with {slot} placeholders; under EXAMPLE: one filled instance

    WHEN writing or using a skill — an agent the harness loads as a skill:
      DO   write it as one EXPORT SKILL <name> AS <role> under agents/library/skills/ — the lines of an AGENT
      DO   import a library skill FROM @main.skills, a harness one FROM @harness.skills — named as the harness knows it
      DO   let the agent use an imported skill freely; add WITH <skill> to say which block it is for
      DO   put WITH right above an IF, a WHEN, a block with lines beneath or a WITH — first in a body for all of it
      DO   put WITH above an EXPORT block to open its body — the skill travels with that block
      DO   write WITH <skill> AS <template> to shape its answer, !WITH <skill> to keep it out of that block
      DO   import the skill in the file that writes the WITH
      NEVER write a WITH on a flow STEP or a COMPUTE block, nor a WITH with nothing below it to decorate
      NEVER write AS on a !WITH — it fails the build

    WHEN binding shapes:
      DO   bind an agent's answers with LENS-OUT <template> and the requests it receives with LENS-IN <template>
      DO   bind a procedure's result or what a STEP hands over with AS <template> on its own line

    WHEN a value must outlive the line that makes it — pick its VAR form:
      DO   write `VAR <name>` in an agent's body for a private variable — only that agent sees it
      DO   write `VAR <name> AS <template>` for a typed variable — it keeps that template for life
      DO   write `VAR <name>:` over an indented block — the block's final outcome becomes its value
      DO   declare a constant or a shared variable as an EXPORT VAR line of a library module — see below
      NEVER write GLOBAL before VAR — the word is reserved and fails the compilation
      NEVER write a constant or a SESSION VAR in an agent's body — both live in a library module
      NEVER declare a name twice for one agent, nor give a private variable an imported variable's name
      NEVER give a variable or a constant the name of a template the agent sees

    WHEN assigning a variable:
      DO   end a DO, ASK, COMPUTE or RUN line with uppercase INTO and the name to store that line's result
      DO   write `RUN <procedure> INTO <name>`, or wrap a lone RUN in a `VAR <name>:` block — both are the same
      DO   write `VAR <name>:` without AS to assign an imported SESSION variable
      DO   reassign with INTO inside a loop
      NEVER write INTO anywhere but the very end of the line — on an ASK, only BY may follow it
      NEVER add AS when assigning an imported variable — `VAR <name> AS <template>:` declares it again
      NEVER declare a VAR inside an UNTIL, a VAR block, a flow STEP, a TEMPLATE, a STORE or a POLICY
      NEVER assign into a constant — INTO takes a declared variable

    WHEN reading a variable:
      DO   write two opening braces, the variable's name, then two closing braces
      DO   add AS and a template after the name, inside the braces, to keep only what fits that template
      NEVER read a field with a dot after the name — field reads are not supported yet
      NEVER give a constant a shaped read, nor a force level to the AS inside the braces
      NEVER read a variable in a shared or builtin library block — it takes its input through LENS-IN
      NEVER read a variable in a TEMPLATE's BODY or EXAMPLE

    WHEN declaring variables shared across agents — one EXPORT VAR line each, filling ap-vars-line:
      DO   write them in a library module, e.g. agents/library/vars/<team>.ap — one top-level line per variable
      DO   write `EXPORT VAR <name> = <value>` for a constant — its value replaces every read at compile time
      DO   write `EXPORT VAR <name>` for a private variable — each importing agent gets its own
      DO   write `EXPORT SESSION VAR <name> [AS <template>]` for a hand-off the session's importing agents share
      DO   IMPORT in every agent, flows.ap or module the exact variables it reads or assigns — @main.vars
      NEVER put a scope word before a constant — SESSION with `= <value>` fails the compilation
      NEVER open a `:` block on an EXPORT VAR line, nor indent it under another block
      NEVER put a secret in a constant — constants are compiled into the output; tools read the environment

    WHEN a condition must be decided by a judge, not by the agent:
      DO   write IF! or UNTIL! — the agent sees the condition, the judge decides it
      DO   write IF!! or UNTIL!! to seal the condition away from the agent
      DO   make the condition read at least one variable — the judge sees only the stored values
      DO   follow IF! or IF!! with ELSE as you would follow IF
      DO   leave the round limit of a checked UNTIL to the apx
      DO   place a check in an agent's body, a role, a procedure, or a flow STEP whose BY names an agent
      DO   confirm the project config sets `checks` — without it IF! reads as IF and IF!! fails
      NEVER write a round count such as "three rounds at most" on a checked UNTIL head
      NEVER put a checked condition at a FLOW's own level or in a team's routing
      NEVER write !IF or !UNTIL — both fail the compilation
      NEVER put a `!` and a star on one condition — a condition has one judge: IF! / IF!! or IF* / IF**

    WHEN a condition does not need a judge:
      DO   keep a plain IF or UNTIL for arithmetic, a count or a one-word decision
      DO   reserve a check for judging stored text — a draft, a plan, a report — against a criterion
      NEVER rely on a check to establish a fact — the judge reads stored text, not the world behind it

    WHEN a line or a condition is better carried out by someone other than the agent:
      DO   write DO for the agent, ASK for a delegate, COMPUTE for a program — a line may raise, never lower
      DO   end an ASK with `INTO <name>` and then `BY <agent>`; without BY the default delegate `quick` answers it
      DO   write IF* or UNTIL* for a delegate, IF** or UNTIL** for a program — the star marks only the condition
      DO   make a starred condition read at least one variable; its branch keeps the enclosing block's executor
      DO   mark a whole procedure PROCEDURE* for a delegate or PROCEDURE** for a program — its lines inherit the mark
      NEVER name a model in the source — the delegate and its model live in the project config

    WHEN an agent keeps data between sessions:
      DO   declare a STORE with TYPE tabeli, LASTS project or session, KEY <slot>, and SLOTS
      DO   work on it inside `IN <store>:` under a WHEN

    WHEN something is worth remembering to act on it faster next time:
      DO   write `MEM <event>` at its level — MEM may, MEM! should, MEM!! must, !MEM never (secrets, credentials)
      DO   shape a memory with an `AS <template>` line indented under the MEM line

    WHEN a procedure is deterministic — the same input always gives the same output:
      DO   give it its contract — LENS-IN <template> for its input when it has one, AS <template> for its result
      DO   mark it PROCEDURE** or write it as a `COMPUTE <name>:` block, run with RUN and IMPORT-able like one
      DO   open a COMPUTE block with ABOUT and the rules on how its script is written
      DO   follow with REQUIRE: (when it refuses), RETURN AS <template>: (the computation), ENSURE: (result checks)
      NEVER leave one of REQUIRE:, RETURN AS and ENSURE: empty or out of that order
      NEVER hand a program a procedure whose result depends on judgment, taste or context
      NEVER write DISTILL — it was removed; mark the procedure PROCEDURE** or write a COMPUTE block

EXPORT AGENT agent-pack-developer AS ap-author:
    ABOUT    Authors and edits agent-pack .ap files — routed to for any task that writes or changes a .ap file
    MANDATE  write correct .ap files by filling the matching block scaffold, then validate with agent-pack bundle all
    OWNS     **/*.ap
